// Which Daily-Performance tiles a mode gets, and what they say.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Two of the six metric tiles are not questions every Daily can be asked, and
// rendering them anyway produced counters that carried no information at all:
//
//   PERFECT SOLVES on Easy could only ever read 0 — Easy deals no power dice, so
//   `correctCount === 3` is arithmetically unreachable. The tile already said so
//   in words, which is better than a zero but is still a whole tile spent
//   telling the player about something that does not exist in the game they are
//   looking at.
//
//   PERFECT SOLVES on Expert could only ever equal the completed count — Expert
//   `requiresPerfectSolve`, so a non-perfect Expert solve is not a worse solve,
//   it is not a solve. A counter whose value is fixed by another counter on the
//   same screen is a duplicate, not a statistic.
//
//   PUZZLE PIECES on Easy is the same shape as the first: a piece is earned by
//   power-dice correctness, and Easy has no power dice, so the number is frozen
//   at whatever Challenge and Expert put there. Shown under an Easy heading it
//   reads as an Easy total, which it is not.
//
// David, 2026-09-04: *"one cant get a perfect, and one is always a perfect."*
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NOTHING HERE HARD-CODES A MODE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Both predicates are derived from `@/game-modes` — `hasPerfectConcept` (does
// this mode deal power dice at all) and `requiresPerfectSolve` (is a perfect
// solve the entry price rather than an achievement). A fourth Daily tier added
// to game-modes gets the right tiles here without this file being edited, which
// is the same argument dailyModeStats.js makes for the labels and the accents.
//
// Everything is DERIVED FROM THE TIMES ROWS, never from a stored counter — this
// project's documented disease (docs/bugs.md: the podium fix, the puzzle-points
// fix, the perfect-solve calendar fix) is a stored aggregate drifting from the
// log it was supposed to summarise.
import { hasPerfectConcept, requiresPerfectSolve } from '@/game-modes';
import * as gameMechanics from '@/game-mechanics';
import {
  normalizeDailyMode,
  filterByMode,
  perfectSolveCountsByMode,
} from './dailyModeStats';

/**
 * Does the Perfect Solves tile mean anything for this Daily?
 *
 * Only where a perfect solve is POSSIBLE but not COMPULSORY — the one band in
 * which the number is a real measure of the player rather than a property of
 * the mode. Today that is Challenge alone; the predicate, not a list, is what
 * says so.
 *
 * @param {string} modeKey
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
export function showsPerfectSolveTile(modeKey) {
  const key = normalizeDailyMode(modeKey);
  return hasPerfectConcept(key) && !requiresPerfectSolve(key);
}

/**
 * Does the Puzzle Pieces tile mean anything for this Daily?
 *
 * Wherever power dice are dealt, since power-dice correctness is what earns a
 * piece (see game-mechanics/puzzlePoints.js). Challenge and Expert today.
 *
 * @param {string} modeKey
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
export function showsPuzzlePieceTile(modeKey) {
  return hasPerfectConcept(normalizeDailyMode(modeKey));
}

/**
 * The Perfect Solves figures for one Daily: the COUNT and the SHARE.
 *
 * David asked for both in one tile — *"have it show perfect solves percentage
 * and totals in the same tab"* — and the share is the half that needed care.
 *
 * ⚠️ BOTH HALVES ARE COUNTED IN DISTINCT DATES, from the SAME rows. The
 * denominator deliberately does not come from `solveCountsByMode` (which counts
 * non-forfeited ROWS) even though the caller already holds it: two rows sharing
 * a date would then divide a date-count by a row-count and the tile could show
 * a share above 100%. One derivation, one unit.
 *
 * A share is only quoted once there is something to divide by; with no solves
 * yet the tile shows the count and no percentage rather than a confident 0%.
 *
 * @param {Array<{date?: string, mode?: string, forfeited?: boolean, equation?: string, usedDiceIndices?: number[], diceColors?: string[]}>|null|undefined} times
 * @param {string} modeKey
 * @returns {{count: number, completed: number, percentage: number|null, applicable: boolean}}
 */
export function perfectSolveSummary(times, modeKey) {
  const key = normalizeDailyMode(modeKey);
  const perfect = perfectSolveCountsByMode(times)[key] ?? { count: 0, applicable: hasPerfectConcept(key) };
  const completed = new Set(
    filterByMode(times, key).filter(t => t && !t.forfeited && t.date).map(t => t.date),
  ).size;
  return {
    count: perfect.count,
    completed,
    percentage: completed > 0 ? Math.round((perfect.count / completed) * 100) : null,
    applicable: perfect.applicable,
  };
}

// Reading an export that may not exist has to be defended, not assumed. Under
// vitest a partially-mocked module is a Proxy that THROWS on any export the
// factory did not declare — so a plain property read here fails every suite
// that mocks '@/game-mechanics' for some other reason, which is most of them.
// A missing export is the EXPECTED state until the rule lands, so it is caught
// and read as 'not declared yet' rather than propagated.
function declaredRuleText() {
  try {
    return /** @type {any} */ (gameMechanics).PUZZLE_PIECE_RULE_TEXT;
  } catch {
    return undefined;
  }
}

/**
 * What the Puzzle Pieces tile says a piece is earned FOR.
 *
 * ⚠️ THIS IS A SEAM, ON PURPOSE. The earning rule belongs to
 * `game-mechanics/puzzlePoints.js`, which another lane is re-cutting right now
 * (an Expert Daily under 37 seconds is to award two pieces rather than one).
 * Hard-coding "Complete Daily Challenge with a Perfect Solve" here — which is
 * what the tile did — makes the caption a SECOND source of truth for the rule,
 * and the two drift the moment the rule moves. That is the same defect class as
 * a stored aggregate.
 *
 * So the caption asks game-mechanics, which exports `PUZZLE_PIECE_RULE_TEXT` —
 * a string, or a function of the mode key when the rule differs per tier. The
 * old "Complete a Daily with a Perfect Solve" fallback was WRONG for Expert
 * (which pays for speed) and was deleted 2026-10-18: with no declared rule the
 * caption is empty rather than a second, stale statement of the rule.
 *
 * @param {string} modeKey
 * @returns {string}
 */
export function puzzlePieceRuleText(modeKey) {
  const key = normalizeDailyMode(modeKey);
  const declared = declaredRuleText();
  if (typeof declared === 'function') return String(declared(key));
  if (typeof declared === 'string' && declared) return declared;
  return '';
}
